      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the probability that the number on the card is both prime and a multiple of 7, we need to determine the prime numbers that are multiples of 7 within the range from 1 to 50.\n\nFirst, identify the prime numbers in the given range. A prime number is a number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself. We need to check which numbers between 1 and 50 are both prime and divisible by 7. \n\nLet's list the multiples of 7 within this range:\n\\[7, 14, 21, 28, 35, 42, 49\\]\n\nNext, we identify which of these numbers are prime:\n- 7 is a prime number.\n- 14 is not a prime number because it can be divided by 1, 2, 7, and 14.\n- 21 is not a prime number because it can be divided by 1, 3, 7, and 21.\n- 28 is not a prime number because it can be divided by 1, 2, 4, 7, 14, and 28.\n- 35 is not a prime number because it can be divided by 1, 5, 7, and 35.\n- 42 is not a prime number because it can be divided by 1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 14, 21, and 42.\n- 49 is not a prime number because it can be divided by 1, 7, and 49.\n\nThe only prime number in this list is 7. Therefore, there is only one number, 7, that satisfies both conditions: it is prime and a multiple of 7.\n\nThe total number of possible outcomes when selecting one card from 50 cards is 50. The number of favorable outcomes (numbers that are both prime and a multiple of 7) is 1. \n\nThe probability \\(P\\) that the number on the card is prime and a multiple of 7 is given by the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of possible outcomes:\n\\[\nP = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of possible outcomes}} = \\frac{1}{50}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{50}}\\).",
